  label: "7. Review and export"
  order: 7
---

Darwin Core is the shared vocabulary that museum databases and portals such as
GBIF use for specimen data. Turning a paper catalog into Darwin Core usually
means retyping it into a spreadsheet, then fixing the spellings, units, dates,
and missing localities by hand. Because you fixed those problems as you entered
the data, NAHPU can build the archive from the linked records directly.

In this tutorial you review the seven specimens, explore their statistics,
export them as a Darwin Core Archive, and read the result.

## 1. Review before you export

Check the records against the paper one last time. Tutorials 3–5 removed most
problems at entry; this review looks for the rest.

| Check | Where | What to look for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Field IDs | Specimens list | `HH436`, `HH437`, `HH442`, `HH453`, `HH456`, `HH523`, `HH529`, each once |
| Names | Each specimen | One spelling per name; *Maxomys* (genus) only for HH 523 |
| Places | Each specimen | Pondok Merah for HH 436–456; Pasir Putih for HH 523 and HH 529 |
| Methods | Each specimen | `Pitfall` for HH 453 and HH 529; `Snap trap` for the others |
| Explained choices | `Remarks` | HH 437, HH 442, HH 456, HH 523, and HH 529 say what was changed and why |
| Gaps | Sites and events | No coordinates for Pasir Putih; no pitfall count for Line 4 |

The gaps are not errors to fix before export. They are honest records of what
the 2014 paper did not capture.

{% aside type="tip" %}
For a quick table to read through, use `Export records` with Darwin Core
headers. See [Export tabular records](/en/how-to-recipes/export-and-print/export-tabular-records/).
{% /aside %}

## 2. Explore the statistics

Before you export, look at the data as a whole. Exploring the record
statistics is a NAHPU best practice. It shows imbalance in what was collected,
shows how consistently parts were sampled, and, in the field, helps you decide
which parts to prioritize when supplies such as tubes or ethanol run low.

On the dashboard, open the `Record statistics` panel and select
`Explore more stats`. The `Summary` view counts the whole project:

![NAHPU Record statistics summary on an iPad: 7 specimens, 19 specimen parts, 5 narratives, 6 recorded sites, 6 events, and a taxonomic breakdown of 2 orders, 3 families, 4 genera, and 5 species](../../../../../assets/images/app-screenshots/tutorials/basic/statistics-summary.webp)

### Check for imbalance

Select `Explore` > `Counts` > `Specimens`. Group by `Species`, break down by
`Sex`, and switch to `Table`:

![NAHPU Explore view on an iPad showing specimens by species and sex as a table](../../../../../assets/images/app-screenshots/tutorials/basic/statistics-species-sex.webp)

Five of the seven specimens are male, and *Maxomys hylomyoides* is the only
species with both sexes. Seven hand-picked records are not a sample, so read
this as a demonstration. With a full catalog, the same table shows whether a
skew is real, for example from trap behavior or season, and whether to note it
before analysis.

### Check part sampling

Select `Part quantity` and group by `Part type`:

![NAHPU Explore view on an iPad showing part quantity by part type: Liver 6, Whole body 4, Skeleton 3, Skin 3, Skull 3](../../../../../assets/images/app-screenshots/tutorials/basic/statistics-parts.webp)

Six of the seven specimens have a liver tissue. The exception is HH 442, whose form
has no tissue circled. During fieldwork, check this table at the end of each
day: it shows whether every specimen got the parts your protocol requires, and
which part types use the most tubes, ethanol, and formalin. When supplies run short, it
helps you decide what to keep collecting.

Every table here has an `Export table` button for reports. See
[Export statistics](/en/usages/export/export-statistics/).

### Read the records in the Data table

To see the records themselves, open `Data table` from `Tools` at the bottom
of the dashboard. Choose what to show, such as `Specimen records`,
`Specimen parts`, `Sites`, `Events`, or `Narrative`, then sort, search, or
filter. This is the parts table for the seven specimens:

![NAHPU Data table on an iPad listing specimen parts with type, count, treatment, cataloger, and field number](../../../../../assets/images/app-screenshots/tutorials/basic/data-table-parts.webp)

The Data table is a quick way to scan for gaps, such as a part with no
treatment or a specimen with no tissue, without opening each record.

## 3. Create the archive

{% steps %}

1. Open the `Dashboard`, open the project menu, and select `Bundle records`.
2. Choose `Darwin Core Archive` as the `Bundle format`. It always uses ZIP.
3. Choose `Selected taxa` and select Mammals.
4. Enter the file name `mt-tujuh-2014` and choose a directory.
5. Review `Package contents`. Expand `occurrence.csv` to see its fields. The
   list also shows any recorded fields that are withheld because they have no
   Darwin Core term.
6. Read any warnings, then select `Create bundle`.

{% /steps %}

NAHPU writes `mt-tujuh-2014.dwca.zip`.

## 4. Read the archive

Unzip the file. It contains:

{% filetree %}
- meta.xml
- eml.xml
- occurrence.csv
- material.csv
- measurement_or_fact.csv
{% /filetree %}

`occurrence.csv` is the core table, with one row per specimen. The other tables
hold parts and measurements and point back to their specimen. `meta.xml`
describes the columns, and `eml.xml` holds the dataset metadata.

### One specimen, one row

This is part of the HH 436 row. Your UUID and Event ID will differ.

| Darwin Core term | Value | Where it came from |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `occurrenceID` | A UUID | Assigned by NAHPU when the record was created |
| `basisOfRecord` | `PreservedSpecimen` | Specimen record |
| `catalogNumber` | `HH436` | Field ID |
| `eventID` | The `TJHH-L1` event | Event |
| `eventDate`, `eventTime` | `2014-09-12`, `16:00` | Specimen |
| `scientificName` | `Lariscus insignis` | Taxon registry |
| `family` | `Sciuridae` | Taxon registry |
| `country`, `stateProvince`, `county` | `Indonesia`, `Jambi`, `Kerinci` | Site |
| `locality` | `Gunung Tujuh, Sungai Jernih, Pondok Merah, beside Lake Gunung Tujuh` | Site |
| `decimalLatitude`, `decimalLongitude` | `-1.70274`, `101.39032` | Site coordinate |
| `minimumElevationInMeters` | `2004` | Site coordinate |
| `habitat` | Type, condition, and description | Site |
| `recordedBy` | `Heru Handika` | Event personnel |
| `identifiedBy` | `Heru Handika` | Specimen |
| `sex`, `lifeStage` | Male, `Subadult` | Specimen |

On paper, many of these values lived in a sheet header, the journal, or the
collector's memory. Here each comes from the record that owns it: the site, the
event, the taxon registry, or the people you entered once.

### What the paper problems became

| Paper | In the archive |
| --- | --- |
| Five spellings of *Maxomys hylomyoides* | One `scientificName` value |
| `01.70274° S`, crossed-out `feet` | `-1.70274`; elevation in meters |
| Blank locality headers | Every row has its own locality |
| Pasir Putih filed under Pondok Merah | HH 523 and HH 529 have the Pasir Putih locality and no coordinates |
| `Line 1` for two methods | Pitfall and snap-trap captures have separate events |
| Heru as collector on every form | `recordedBy` names the person from the event |
| `SA` and `AL` | Rows in `material.csv` with a type and treatment, such as `Skull`, `Whole body` in `Formalin`, or `Liver` in `ETOH` |
| `TTL 238`, `TL 84`, … with no units | Rows in `measurement_or_fact.csv` with names and units, such as `total length`, `238`, `mm` |

{% aside type="caution" title="Validate before you publish" %}
Before you send the archive to a museum or portal, run it through a validator,
such as the [GBIF data validator](https://www.gbif.org/tools/data-validator),
and fix what it reports in NAHPU, not in the CSV files. Then export again.
{% /aside %}

## What you have done

Across the seven Basic tutorials you:

- read a paper catalog critically and listed its problems;
- created a project with its people and a clean taxon registry;
- turned one locality header into six sites, and the journal's trap notes into
  six events;
- entered seven specimens, choosing the best-supported value where the paper
  disagreed with itself and explaining each choice;
- corrected and entered the field journal, linked to the same sites;
- checked sample balance and part sampling with record statistics and the
  Data table;
- exported a standard Darwin Core Archive from the linked records.

Young Heru's catalog was better than most: it has standard measurements, trap
lines, and a journal. NAHPU does not replace that care. It makes the links
between place, time, method, people, and specimen part of the record, so they
are not lost when the notebook is closed.

